kubeos-storage (1.0.0-16) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The sweep now says when it declines to act because the volume record belongs to a
    different cluster. Without that line "the guard worked" and "the sweep is broken" look
    exactly the same in the journal - and this is the branch that keeps a removed node's
    data alive.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:40:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-15) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The cluster identity the sweep relies on is now read from LOCAL files instead of the
    API. 1.0.0-12 asked for the kube-system namespace UID, which this service's own narrow
    ClusterRole forbids ("User system:storage-provisioner cannot get resource namespaces"),
    so the round aborted before provisioning and every new claim stayed Pending - caught
    on the test cluster within minutes, and exactly the kind of regression a permission
    assumption creates. The identity is now the cluster CA every node holds
    (/etc/kubeos-dashboard/ca.crt) plus the API server its kubeconfig names: both are
    cluster-specific, both are local, and the CA is what changes when a node becomes its
    own cluster again (verified on kubeos2: rewritten at the standalone rebuild, adopted
    from the cluster again when it re-joined).

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:20:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The sweep timer schedules its first run relative to when IT becomes active
    (OnActiveSec) instead of relative to boot (OnBootSec). Measured 2026-11-20 on all
    three nodes immediately after 1.0.0-13 was installed: the timer was `active` and
    `systemctl list-timers` answered `next -`, so nothing would ever have run until the
    next reboot - OnBootSec was already in the past, and OnUnitActiveSec only repeats
    after a first run that never happened.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:14:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The sweep timer now actually starts when the package is installed. A .wants link
    shipped in a package starts a timer only when timers.target is next activated, i.e. at
    the next boot: measured 2026-11-20 right after installing 1.0.0-12, the unit, the link
    and `systemctl cat` were all correct while `systemctl list-timers` showed nothing, so
    the sweep would not have existed until a reboot. postinst starts it now (and warns,
    rather than failing, if it cannot); the shipped link keeps it enabled at every boot.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:12:00 +0530

kubeos-storage (1.0.0-12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * SECURITY/DATA: a removed node no longer destroys its persistent volumes. The orphan
    sweep deleted every `pvc-*` directory that the CURRENT cluster does not reference, and
    the one-node cluster a removed node rebuilds for itself references nothing - so the
    sweep wiped the data of the cluster that node had just left. Measured 2026-11-20 on the
    three-node test cluster: `reclaimed /opt/local-path-provisioner/pvc-8ba46de5… (no PVC
    and no PV references it)` at the moment the standalone rebuild started, and afterwards
    the pod restarted "successfully" on an empty volume, while the user guide promises the
    data is left untouched. The sweep now only removes a directory this cluster is on
    record as having owned; that record (`/var/lib/kubeos/storage-provisioned`) names the
    cluster it belongs to by the kube-system UID, because the file itself survives the
    rebuild and a bare list of names would still point at the old volumes. A directory of
    unknown origin is left alone: a leak is recoverable, deleted data is not.
  * A deleted claim whose volume lived on a WORKER now frees its disk. Only the control
    plane ran the provisioner, so the directory on the worker that hosted the volume was
    never reclaimed (measured 2026-11-20: 0 PVs, 0 PVCs, the directory still there).
    New `kubeos-storage-gc.service` + `.timer`: a oneshot every five minutes on EVERY
    node, running the provisioner with `--sweep-only`, which reclaims and never
    provisions - a worker still cannot create volumes.
  * The provisioner picks its cluster identity from `/etc/kubernetes/storage-provisioner.conf`
    on a control plane and falls back to `/etc/kubeos-dashboard/kubeconfig` on a worker,
    which is what lets the sweep run there at all.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 09:10:00 +0530

